---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
---

<Base>
  <div class="umi-region umi-pad" id="umi-api">
    <div id="umi-columns">
      <nav id="umi-toc">
        <h2>Table of Contents</h2>
        <ul id="toc-list"></ul>
      </nav>
      <main id="umi-content">
        <h1>Umi‑Time API Reference</h1>
        <section id="introduction">
          <h2>Introduction</h2>

          <p>
            Umi‑Time is a UTC-based representation standard that expresses the
            UTC day as a continuous 360‑unit cycle called <em>umi</em>. Each umi
            corresponds to four minutes of UTC, providing a simple geometric
            representation of the daily cycle that is easy to compute and reason
            about.
          </p>

          <p>
            The library mirrors the Umi‑Time architecture. UTC is represented as
            a Layer 1 Umi‑Time value, optional longitude information provides
            Layer 2 local interpretation, and solar‑geometry helpers provide
            Layer 3 solar context.
          </p>

          <p>
            The core of the library is the <code>UmiTime</code> class. A
            <code>UmiTime</code> instance is anchored to a specific UTC moment and
            an approximate observer location, represented by latitude and longitude
            with uncertainty. From this single value, the class provides derived components:
            civil‑date fields (<code>year</code>, <code>month</code>, <code
              >day</code
            >) and Umi‑Time fields (<code>umi</code>, <code>second</code>,
            <code>millisecond</code>). Immutability helpers (<code>withUTC</code
            >,
            <code>withLatitude</code>, <code>withLongitude</code>) allow
            constructing new instances with updated context.
          </p>

          <p>
            The library includes solar‑geometry helpers that operate on
            <code>UmiTime</code> instances. These derived properties compute quantities
            such as solar declination, equation of time, solar altitude and azimuth,
            sunrise, and sunset, using the UTC timestamp together with the observer’s
            latitude and longitude. These values support Layer 3 solar‑context features
            and do not affect the underlying Umi‑Time value or Layer 2 local interpretation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Umi‑Time also supports formatting and parsing. The
            <code>format(spec)</code> method produces Umi‑Time strings according to
            predefined or custom format specifiers, and
            <code>UmiTime.parse(input, opts)</code> constructs a
            <code>UmiTime</code> instance from a string, optionally guided by explicit
            latitude and longitude hints.
          </p>

          <p>
            This API reference documents the <code>UmiTime</code> class, the solar‑
            geometry helpers, the formatting and parsing functions, the predefined
            format constants, and the supporting utility functions exported by the
            library. It serves as the authoritative guide for using Umi‑Time in applications,
            tools, and language bindings.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="core-concepts">
          <h2>Core Concepts</h2>

          <section id="concept-umi">
            <h3>Umi</h3>
            <p>
              Umi‑Time divides the UTC day into 360 equal units called <em
                >umi</em
              >. Each umi corresponds to four minutes of UTC. This creates a
              simple, uniform geometric representation of the daily cycle.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="concept-utc-location">
            <h3>UTC and Location</h3>
            <p>
              Every Umi‑Time value is anchored to a specific UTC moment.
              Latitude and longitude provide optional context for local
              interpretation and solar calculations. Longitude may be used to
              derive Layer 2 local context, while latitude and longitude
              together are used for Layer 3 solar‑context calculations such as
              solar altitude, azimuth, sunrise, and sunset. These geographic
              values do not affect the underlying Umi‑Time value.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="concept-segments">
            <h3>Day Segments</h3>
            <p>
              Day segments divide the local Umi‑Day into a fixed number of equal
              intervals. They provide a compact Layer 2 local-context
              representation by identifying where a Umi‑Time value falls within
              the observer's local day without altering the underlying Umi‑Time
              value.
            </p>

            <p>
              Different segment systems may use different numbers of divisions.
              For example, the day‑phase system divides the local Umi‑Day into
              24 equal segments of 15 umi each, while other segment schemes may
              use coarser or finer subdivisions. All segment systems are derived
              from the same longitude‑based local interpretation.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="concept-refinement">
            <h3>Refinement</h3>
            <p>
              Refinement is an optional offset inside a day segment. It
              describes how far the current umi value lies from the start of its
              segment. Refinement is used for more precise contextual labeling
              and does not modify the underlying Umi‑Time value.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="concept-strings">
            <h3>Umi‑Time Strings</h3>
            <p>
              Umi‑Time strings provide textual representations of Umi‑Time
              values. Formats may include date prefixes, segment codes,
              refinement suffixes, and longitude annotations. These strings can
              be parsed back into fully constructed <code>UmiTime</code> instances.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="concept-umitime-class">
            <h3>The UmiTime Class</h3>
            <p>
              The <code>UmiTime</code> class represents a UTC moment together with
              an approximate observer location. It provides derived civil‑date fields,
              Umi‑Time fields (<code>umi</code>, <code>second</code>,
              <code>millisecond</code>), solar geometry, segment helpers, and
              formatting and parsing capabilities. All API functions operate on
              or produce <code>UmiTime</code> instances.
            </p>
          </section>
        </section>
        <section id="umitime-class">
          <h2>UmiTime Class</h2>

          <p>
            The <code>UmiTime</code> class represents a single moment in time anchored
            to a UTC timestamp and an approximate observer location. All Umi‑Time
            values, solar‑geometry context, segment information, and formatted string
            representations are derived from this core data.
          </p>

          <h3>Constructor</h3>

          <Code
            code={`new UmiTime(
  utc: Date,
  latitude: ApproximateValue = { value: 0, uncertainty: 90 },
  longitude: ApproximateValue = { value: 0, uncertainty: 180 },
)`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />

          <p>
            Creates a new <code>UmiTime</code> instance. The <code>utc</code> value
            is required. Latitude and longitude are optional and may include uncertainty.
            These geographic values are used only for solar‑geometry calculations
            and do not affect the underlying Umi‑Time value.
          </p>

          <h3>Properties</h3>

          <h4>utc</h4>
          <p>
            A <code>Date</code> object representing the underlying UTC timestamp for
            this Umi‑Time value. All derived fields are computed from this moment.
          </p>

          <h4>latitude</h4>
          <p>
            An <code>ApproximateValue</code> describing the observer’s latitude. Used
            for solar‑geometry calculations. May include uncertainty.
          </p>

          <h4>longitude</h4>
          <p>
            An <code>ApproximateValue</code> describing the observer’s longitude.
            Used for solar‑geometry calculations and optional day‑segment inference.
            May include uncertainty.
          </p>

          <h3>Immutability Helpers</h3>

          <p>
            These methods return new <code>UmiTime</code> instances with updated values.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={`withUTC(utc: Date): UmiTime
withLatitude(value: number, uncertainty?: number): UmiTime
withLongitude(value: number, uncertainty?: number): UmiTime`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />

          <h3>Derived Civil‑Date Fields</h3>

          <ul>
            <li><code>year: number</code></li>
            <li><code>month: number</code></li>
            <li><code>day: number</code></li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            These values are extracted directly from the underlying UTC
            timestamp.
          </p>

          <h3>Derived Umi‑Time Fields</h3>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <code>umi: number</code> — the umi position within the 360‑umi UTC day
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>second: number</code> — the second within the current umi (0–239)
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>millisecond: number</code> — the millisecond within the current
              second
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            These values are computed from the UTC timestamp using the Umi‑Time
            day structure.
          </p>

          <h3>Solar Geometry</h3>

          <p>
            These getters compute solar‑related quantities for the current UTC
            moment and observer location. Some calculations require sufficiently
            precise latitude and longitude values and may throw
            <code>UmiTimeError</code> if the uncertainty is too large.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <Code
                code={`get solarDeclination(): number`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Computes the Sun’s declination angle for this moment.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`get equationOfTime(): number`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Computes the equation of time, describing the difference between apparent
              and mean solar time.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`get solarPosition(): { altitude: number; azimuth: number }`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Computes the Sun’s altitude and azimuth for the observer’s location.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code code={`get sunrise(): number`} lang="ts" theme="dracula" />
              Returns the umi of sunrise for the observer’s location.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code code={`get sunset(): number`} lang="ts" theme="dracula" />
              Returns the umi of sunset for the observer’s location.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            These values are computed using the UTC timestamp together with the
            observer’s latitude and longitude. Some calculations require
            sufficiently precise geographic values and may throw <code
              >UmiTimeError</code
            > if the uncertainty is too large.
          </p>

          <h3>Day Segment Helpers</h3>

          <Code
            code={`segmentIndex(total: number): number
segmentRefinement(total: number): number`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />

          <p>
            These helpers divide the 360‑umi UTC day into equal parts and
            compute the current segment and the offset within that segment. They
            do not modify the underlying Umi‑Time value.
          </p>

          <h4>Format Tokens</h4>

          <p>
            Tokens fall into two categories: <strong>variable tokens</strong>,
            which insert computed values, and <strong>literal tokens</strong>,
            which appear exactly as written in the output.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Date tokens (variable)</strong>
              <ul>
                <li><code>yyyy</code> — 4‑digit year</li>
                <li><code>MM</code> — 2‑digit month</li>
                <li><code>dd</code> — 2‑digit day of month</li>
              </ul>
            </li>

            <li>
              <strong>Umi‑Time tokens (variable)</strong>
              <ul>
                <li><code>uuu</code> — umi (000–359), zero‑padded</li>
                <li>
                  <code>sss</code> — second within the umi (000–239), zero‑padded
                </li>
              </ul>
            </li>

            <li>
              <strong>Literal tokens</strong>
              <ul>
                <li><code>U</code> — literal prefix indicating Umi‑Time</li>
                <li>
                  <code>:</code> — literal separator between umi and seconds
                </li>
              </ul>
            </li>

            <li>
              <strong>Segment tokens (variable)</strong>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <code>G24</code> — segment suffix (e.g., <code>S19/24</code>)
                </li>
              </ul>
            </li>

            <li>
              <strong>Refinement tokens (variable)</strong>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <code>+</code> — refinement offset inside the segment (e.g., <code
                    >+12</code
                  >)
                </li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The predefined specifiers below combine these tokens into commonly
            used Umi‑Time formats.
          </p>

          <p>The library provides the following predefined specifiers:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <Code
                code={`UMI_COMPACT = "Uuuusss"`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Compact Umi‑Time with a leading <code>U</code>. No date, no
              segment, no refinement.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`UMI_COMPACT_BARE = "uuusss"`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Compact form without the leading <code>U</code>.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`UMI_EXTENDED = "Uuuu:sss"`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Extended compact form with a colon separator.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`UMI_EXTENDED_BARE = "uuu:sss"`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Extended compact form without the leading <code>U</code>.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`UMI_DATE_TIME = "yyyy-MM-ddUuuusss"`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Date + compact Umi‑Time. Closest to ISO‑8601 in structure.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`UMI_DATE_TIME_EXT = "yyyy-MM-ddUuuu:sss"`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Date + extended Umi‑Time. More human‑readable variant of
              <code>UMI_DATE_TIME</code>.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`UMI_CANONICAL_FULL = "yyyy-MM-ddUuuusssG24+"`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Full canonical Umi‑Time including segment and refinement suffixes.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`UMI_CANONICAL_FULL_EXT = "yyyy-MM-ddUuuu:sssG24+"`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Extended canonical form including segment and refinement.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Parsing</h3>

          <Code
            code={`static parse(
  input: string,
  opts?: { latitude?: number; longitude?: number },
): UmiTime`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />

          <p>
            Parses a Umi‑Time string and returns a fully constructed
            <code>UmiTime</code> instance. The parser recognizes a small set of built‑in
            patterns directly, without requiring an explicit format specifier. It
            accepts:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Basic Umi‑Time:</strong>
              <code>uuusss</code> (e.g. <code>042180</code>)
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Extended Umi‑Time:</strong>
              <code>uuu:sss</code> (e.g. <code>042:180</code>)
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Umi‑only:</strong>
              <code>uuu</code> (e.g. <code>042</code>, interpreted as <code
                >042000</code
              >)
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Optional leading U:</strong> any of the above may start with
              <code>U</code> (e.g. <code>U042180</code>)
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Optional date prefix:</strong>
              <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code> before the Umi‑Time portion
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Optional segment suffix:</strong>
              <code>Sindex/total</code> (e.g. <code>S3/24</code>)
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Optional refinement suffix:</strong>
              <code>+nnn</code> (e.g. <code>+12</code>)
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Optional longitude suffix:</strong>
              <code>+ddd</code> or <code>-ddd</code> (e.g. <code>+123</code>)
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            If the string does not encode longitude, an explicit
            <code>opts.longitude</code> value or a segment/refinement suffix is used
            to infer an approximate longitude. If no latitude is provided via
            <code>opts.latitude</code>, a default approximate latitude is used.
          </p>

          <p>
            Umi and second values are validated; out‑of‑range values or
            malformed input produce a <code>ParseError</code>.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the full definition of Umi‑Time textual formats, see the
            <a href="/spec#string-formats">Umi‑Time Specification</a>. The
            parser accepts all standard forms described there.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="solar-geometry">
          <h2>Solar Geometry Helpers</h2>

          <p>
            The Umi‑Time library provides a small set of standalone functions
            for computing solar‑related quantities. These helpers operate on UTC
            timestamps and geographic coordinates, and are used internally by
            the
            <code>UmiTime</code> class’s solar‑geometry getters. They may also be
            used directly in applications that need lower‑level access to solar calculations.
          </p>

          <h3>Angle Conversion</h3>

          <p>Basic helpers for converting between radians and degrees.</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <Code
                code={`toDeg(rad: number): number`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Converts radians to degrees.
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`toRad(deg: number): number`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              Converts degrees to radians.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3>UT Umi From Date</h3>

          <p>
            Computes the umi position for a given UTC timestamp. This is the
            same calculation used internally by <code>UmiTime</code> to derive its
            <code>umi</code> getter.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={`utUmiFromDate(utc: Date): number`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />

          <h3>Solar Altitude</h3>

          <p>
            Computes the Sun’s altitude angle for a given UTC moment and
            observer location. Altitude is measured in degrees above the
            horizon.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={`solarAltitude(
  utc: Date,
  latitude: number,
  longitude: number,
): number`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />

          <h3>Hour Angle</h3>

          <p>
            Computes the solar hour angle for a given UTC moment and observer
            location. The hour angle describes the Sun’s position relative to
            local solar noon.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={`getHourAngle(
  utc: Date,
  longitude: number,
  equationOfTime: number,
): number`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />
        </section>
        <section id="approximate-value">
          <h2>ApproximateValue</h2>

          <p>
            <code>ApproximateValue</code> represents a numeric value together with
            an uncertainty radius. It is used for geographic coordinates in
            <code>UmiTime</code>, allowing the library to express both exact and
            approximate positions. Solar‑geometry calculations rely on these
            values to determine whether the observer’s location is sufficiently
            precise for computing solar context.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={`interface ApproximateValue {
  value: number;        // central value
  uncertainty: number;  // ± range around the value
}`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />

          <h3>Semantics</h3>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>value</strong> — the estimated latitude or longitude.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>uncertainty</strong> — the radius of the interval around
              <code>value</code> within which the true coordinate may lie.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            An uncertainty of <code>0</code> indicates an exact coordinate. Larger
            uncertainties represent increasingly approximate positions. Examples:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <Code
                code={`{ value: 34.9, uncertainty: 0 }`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              — exact coordinate
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`{ value: 120, uncertainty: 15 }`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              — approximate longitude
            </li>

            <li>
              <Code
                code={`{ value: 0, uncertainty: 180 }`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
              — completely unknown longitude
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Usage in UmiTime</h3>

          <p>
            <code>ApproximateValue</code> is used for both <code>latitude</code> and
            <code>longitude</code> in the <code>UmiTime</code> constructor and in
            parsing. When a Umi‑Time string does not specify location, the parser
            constructs approximate values using default uncertainties:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>latitude:</strong>
              <Code
                code={`{ value: 0, uncertainty: 90 }`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
            </li>

            <li>
              <strong>longitude:</strong>
              <Code
                code={`{ value: 0, uncertainty: 180 }`}
                lang="ts"
                theme="dracula"
              />
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Segment and refinement suffixes (<code>Sindex/total</code> and
            <code>+refinement</code>) reduce longitude uncertainty by narrowing
            the inferred solar segment.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="errors">
          <h2>Errors</h2>

          <p>
            Umi‑Time defines two error classes used throughout parsing and
            solar‑geometry evaluation. Both extend the built‑in <code
              >Error</code
            >
            type and provide descriptive messages indicating what went wrong.
          </p>

          <h3>ParseError</h3>

          <Code
            code={`class ParseError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "ParseError";
  }
}`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />

          <p>
            <code>ParseError</code> is thrown when a Umi‑Time string cannot be parsed.
            This includes malformed input, invalid umi or second ranges, or unrecognized
            combinations of date, segment, refinement, or longitude suffixes.
          </p>

          <p>Examples of conditions that produce a <code>ParseError</code>:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>umi outside <code>0–359</code></li>
            <li>second outside <code>0–239</code></li>
            <li>
              invalid Umi‑Time portion (neither <code>uuu:sss</code> nor
              <code>uuusss</code>)
            </li>
            <li>malformed segment suffix (e.g., <code>S3/x</code>)</li>
            <li>malformed refinement suffix (e.g., <code>+abc</code>)</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>UmiTimeError</h3>

          <Code
            code={`class UmiTimeError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "UmiTimeError";
  }
}`}
            lang="ts"
            theme="dracula"
          />

          <p>
            <code>UmiTimeError</code> is thrown when a valid <code>UmiTime</code
            >
            instance cannot perform a requested computation. This occurs when solar‑geometry
            calculations require more precise geographic information than the instance
            contains.
          </p>

          <p>Common reasons for <code>UmiTimeError</code>:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>latitude uncertainty too large for solar altitude/azimuth</li>
            <li>
              longitude uncertainty too large for sunrise/sunset computation
            </li>
            <li>segment/refinement inference insufficiently precise</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            These errors indicate that the caller should provide more precise
            <code>latitude</code> or <code>longitude</code> values, either via
            <code>withLatitude()</code>, <code>withLongitude()</code>, or by
            supplying explicit coordinates when parsing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="module-parity-matrix">
          <h2>Module Parity Matrix</h2>

          <p>
            Umi‑Time is designed as a multi‑language library. Each language
            binding implements the same conceptual modules, though some bindings
            may not yet support all modules. The table below summarizes current
            parity across official implementations.
          </p>

          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Module</th>
                <th>JavaScript / TypeScript</th>
                <th>Python</th>
                <th>Rust</th>
                <th>C++</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Core UmiTime Class</td>
                <td>✔ Implemented</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Formatting API</td>
                <td>✔ Implemented</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Parsing API</td>
                <td>✔ Implemented</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Solar Geometry API</td>
                <td>✔ Implemented</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Segment API</td>
                <td>✔ Implemented</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
                <td>⧗ Planned</td>
              </tr>
